    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_f058a99b3d5393e79db7e2dc0d391c52e522388d0498dd06f9e3e73c1d0025b4",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "For this specific smoothing function u and Fourier convention, the regular symbol is created by two controlled cancellations. Near theta=0, the j=0 cosine-integral expansion cancels the logarithmic characteristic-polynomial singularity and leaves b(0)=exp(2k(log log X+gamma)) times 1+O_k(1/log X); the j != 0 periodic images contribute uniformly only O(1/log X). Meanwhile the nonzero Fourier coefficients k v(exp(n/log X))/n encode a smoothed transition for n of order log X, not a hard cutoff, and the zeroth coefficient vanishes, so the Szego exponential receives no term linear in N from the mean of log b. Periodization therefore gains Toeplitz compatibility with a quantified loss only for the stated kernel and support assumptions.",
      "parent_sources": [
